       "$sec^2(x)$"
      ]
     ],
     "explain": "Learn these three by heart."
    },
    {
     "id": "T2",
     "type": "match",
     "text": "Match each function to its derivative.",
     "pairs": [
      [
       "$sec(x)$",
       "$sec(x)tan(x)$"
      ],
      [
       "$csc(x)$",
       "$-csc(x)cot(x)$"
      ],
      [
       "$cot(x)$",
       "$-csc^2(x)$"
      ]
     ],
     "explain": "The co-functions get the minus signs."
    },
    {
     "id": "T3",
     "type": "tf",
     "text": "$frac{d}{dx} cos(x) = sin(x)$",
     "answer": false,
     "explain": "It is $-sin(x)$: cosine starts falling right after $x = 0$."
    },
    {
     "id": "T4",
     "type": "mc",
     "text": "Which limit is the key fact behind $frac{d}{dx} sin(x) = cos(x)$?",
     "options": [
      "$lim[x->0] frac{sin(x)}{x} = 1$",
      "$lim[x->0] sin(x) = 1$",
      "$lim[x->infty] frac{sin(x)}{x} = 1$",
      "$lim[x->0] frac{x}{sin(x)} = 0$"
     ],
     "answer": 0,
     "explain": "It comes from the definition of the derivative with $sin(x + h)$."
    },
    {
     "id": "T5",
     "type": "mc",
     "text": "What is $frac{d^47}{dx^47} cos(x)$?",
     "options": [
      "$sin(x)$",
      "$cos(x)$",
      "$-sin(x)$",
      "$-cos(x)$"
     ],
     "answer": 0,
     "explain": "47 = 44 + 3. After 44 derivatives you are back at $cos(x)$; three more: $-sin(x)$, $-cos(x)$, $sin(x)$."
    },
    {
     "id": "T6",
     "type": "mc",
     "text": "How would you find $frac{d}{dx} tan(x)$ if you forgot it?",
     "options": [
      "Write $tan(x) = frac{sin(x)}{cos(x)}$ and use the quotient rule",
      "Use the power rule",
      "Take the derivative of the top and the bottom separately",
      "It cannot be done"
     ],
     "answer": 0,
     "explain": "$frac{cos(x)cos(x) - sin(x)(-sin(x))}{cos^2(x)} = frac{1}{cos^2(x)} = sec^2(x)$"
    }
   ]
  },
